1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,510
Ask an A.I. one simple question.

2
00:00:02,750 --> 00:00:04,710
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,540
Before it reads your question,

4
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:08,720
the machine chops it into pieces.

5
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:10,810
In English... just eight.

6
00:00:10,930 --> 00:00:12,580
Now ask it in Hindi.

7
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:13,430
On DeepSeek,

8
00:00:13,430 --> 00:00:16,070
the famous Chinese A.I.... eighteen.

9
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:17,340
More than double.

10
00:00:17,410 --> 00:00:18,980
Marathi, twenty.

11
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:20,930
Telugu, twenty-five.

12
00:00:21,060 --> 00:00:21,920
And in those two,

13
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,410
it even cuts some of your letters in half.

14
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,060
So can India build an A.I. of its own?

15
00:00:29,090 --> 00:00:31,480
One that speaks your language best?

16
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,690
Here's a clue for you.

17
00:00:32,810 --> 00:00:34,180
Ask it in Chinese.

18
00:00:34,210 --> 00:00:35,420
Seven pieces.

19
00:00:35,450 --> 00:00:36,760
Fewer than English.

20
00:00:36,870 --> 00:00:38,150
You see what's going on?

21
00:00:38,190 --> 00:00:42,200
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who built it.

22
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:43,630
And it's not just pride.

23
00:00:43,720 --> 00:00:46,590
Your apps pay these machines by the piece.

24
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,610
More pieces means a bigger bill,

25
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:50,640
and a slower answer for you.

26
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:52,630
Now think back to last November.

27
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,010
Chat G.P.T.'s paid plan, called Go,

28
00:00:55,010 --> 00:00:57,830
became free in India for a whole year.

29
00:00:57,910 --> 00:00:59,360
Maybe you signed up too.

30
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:03,060
OpenAI says India is now one of its biggest markets.

31
00:01:03,160 --> 00:01:05,250
For the first people who signed up,

32
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:07,810
that free year runs out... about now.

33
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:08,900
So when yours ends,

34
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,200
whose machine will you be talking to?

35
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:12,740
One built in America?

36
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:14,190
One built in China?

37
00:01:14,220 --> 00:01:15,510
Or one built here?

38
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:17,930
To see why India doesn't have its own yet,

39
00:01:17,930 --> 00:01:20,160
you have to go back to one Monday.

40
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:21,800
The twenty-seventh of January,

41
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:23,120
twenty twenty-five.

42
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:27,880
A small Chinese lab called DeepSeek has just put out a free A.I.

43
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:28,820
By that Monday,

44
00:01:28,820 --> 00:01:33,290
it's beaten Chat G.P.T. in downloads on Apple's App Store in America.

45
00:01:34,070 --> 00:01:37,440
DeepSeek says the final training run of its base model

46
00:01:37,440 --> 00:01:40,460
cost about five and a half million dollars.

47
00:01:40,580 --> 00:01:43,250
The American giants, you may remember,

48
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,140
were spending billions.

49
00:01:45,170 --> 00:01:45,660
That day,

50
00:01:45,660 --> 00:01:51,140
the American chip company Nvidia loses around five hundred and ninety billion dollars in value.

51
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:53,830
That's about fifty lakh crore rupees.

52
00:01:54,180 --> 00:01:56,040
Think about that number for a second.

53
00:01:56,190 --> 00:01:59,920
It's roughly the size of India's whole Union Budget for

54
00:01:59,920 --> 00:02:03,400
that year... gone from one company in one day.

55
00:02:04,620 --> 00:02:06,290
Three days later, in Delhi,

56
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:08,750
India's I.T. minister, Ashwini Vaishnaw,

57
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:10,090
makes a promise.

58
00:02:10,210 --> 00:02:15,900
India will build its own A.I. model... and he hopes it will be ready within six months.

59
00:02:16,010 --> 00:02:18,370
So did India keep that promise?

60
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:19,300
You'll see.

61
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:20,640
And around minute ten,

62
00:02:20,640 --> 00:02:24,860
I'll run that same chopping test for you again... on an Indian machine.

63
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:26,460
That's where it gets interesting.

64
00:02:26,580 --> 00:02:27,110
But first,

65
00:02:27,110 --> 00:02:30,680
you need to know what's actually inside one of these machines.

66
00:02:30,710 --> 00:02:32,430
Because it isn't magic.

67
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:33,780
It's three things.

68
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,710
Picture a child who has read every book in a giant library.

69
00:02:37,910 --> 00:02:41,310
You start a sentence... and the child guesses your next word.

70
00:02:41,440 --> 00:02:42,430
Then the next one.

71
00:02:42,480 --> 00:02:43,400
Then the next.

72
00:02:43,540 --> 00:02:45,750
That's really all a chatbot does.

73
00:02:45,780 --> 00:02:48,570
It guesses the next piece, again and again,

74
00:02:48,570 --> 00:02:51,250
so well that you feel it's thinking.

75
00:02:51,330 --> 00:02:52,890
So if you want to build one,

76
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:54,430
you need three things.

77
00:02:54,460 --> 00:02:55,820
A library to read.

78
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:57,830
A reading room where it can read.

79
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,410
And teachers who know how to train the child.

80
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:02,660
The library is words —

81
00:03:02,660 --> 00:03:04,460
trillions of pieces of text,

82
00:03:04,460 --> 00:03:06,290
like the ones you type.

83
00:03:06,520 --> 00:03:09,570
The reading room is chips, thousands of them,

84
00:03:09,570 --> 00:03:12,160
running day and night for months.

85
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:13,430
And the teachers?

86
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:14,780
They're engineers.

87
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,550
India has the teachers.

88
00:03:16,580 --> 00:03:17,820
You know this already.

89
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:21,640
Indian engineers write software for companies all over the world.

90
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:25,400
So your missing pieces are the library and the reading room.

91
00:03:25,620 --> 00:03:27,230
Let's start with the library.

92
00:03:27,510 --> 00:03:29,860
Almost nobody tells you about this part.

93
00:03:30,080 --> 00:03:32,750
A lot of A.I. learns from the internet.

94
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:36,810
One of the biggest free copies of the web is called Common Crawl.

95
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:38,940
Many A.I. models learned from it,

96
00:03:38,940 --> 00:03:43,350
including an early model from the makers of the Chat G.P.T. you use.

97
00:03:43,510 --> 00:03:45,220
So we took its latest copy,

98
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:47,250
from October twenty twenty-six —

99
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:49,340
more than two billion web pages —

100
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,160
and checked its own count of languages.

101
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:53,060
English?

102
00:03:53,090 --> 00:03:55,940
Forty-two out of every hundred pages.

103
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:57,820
Now, can you guess Hindi?

104
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,660
For every one page in Hindi... there are about two hundred pages in English.

105
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:06,520
And Hindi is the mother tongue of more than fifty crore people.

106
00:04:06,650 --> 00:04:07,440
Marathi?

107
00:04:07,490 --> 00:04:08,840
About fifteen hundred.

108
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:09,630
Telugu?

109
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:11,560
About two thousand three hundred.

110
00:04:11,590 --> 00:04:15,390
And each of those is the mother tongue of around eight crore people,

111
00:04:15,390 --> 00:04:16,410
maybe yours.

112
00:04:16,570 --> 00:04:21,790
All of India's languages together make up less than one page in every hundred.

113
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:22,960
Think about it.

114
00:04:22,990 --> 00:04:27,610
More than one in every six people on Earth lives in your country.

115
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:30,010
But in the library these machines read,

116
00:04:30,010 --> 00:04:32,410
your language is almost invisible.

117
00:04:33,050 --> 00:04:35,820
And India's job is harder than China's.

118
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:38,980
China's A.I. mostly needs one written language.

119
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:41,920
Your country's Constitution lists twenty-two,

120
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,930
written in about a dozen different scripts.

121
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:46,980
Each one needs its own shelf.

122
00:04:47,150 --> 00:04:50,790
And that's why your question cost so much more in Hindi,

123
00:04:50,790 --> 00:04:52,380
Marathi and Telugu.

124
00:04:52,470 --> 00:04:53,700
Remember the chopper?

125
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:56,610
It learns its pieces from this same library.

126
00:04:56,690 --> 00:04:58,600
Words it sees all the time,

127
00:04:58,600 --> 00:05:00,080
like rain or village,

128
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:02,330
become one neat piece each.

129
00:05:02,360 --> 00:05:04,740
Your words, which it hardly ever sees,

130
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:06,380
get chopped into bits.

131
00:05:06,410 --> 00:05:08,420
Sometimes into half a letter.

132
00:05:08,520 --> 00:05:09,410
Here's the rule.

133
00:05:09,520 --> 00:05:12,030
A machine learns from what it reads.

134
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,270
And what goes wrong?

135
00:05:13,310 --> 00:05:16,580
If your language is only a tiny corner of the library,

136
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:20,020
the machine learns it last... and charges you more.

137
00:05:20,050 --> 00:05:23,370
So someone has to build the Indian part of the library.

138
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:25,840
Page by page, voice by voice.

139
00:05:25,870 --> 00:05:28,280
And that's slow, expensive work.

140
00:05:28,410 --> 00:05:30,030
And India has started.

141
00:05:30,150 --> 00:05:32,790
A government data shelf for Indian builders,

142
00:05:32,790 --> 00:05:33,810
called A.I. Kosh,

143
00:05:33,810 --> 00:05:37,050
now holds more than sixteen thousand collections.

144
00:05:37,080 --> 00:05:39,090
But words are only half of it.

145
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:40,740
Even with a perfect library,

146
00:05:40,740 --> 00:05:42,640
you need a place to read it.

147
00:05:42,670 --> 00:05:45,040
And this is where India hits a wall.

148
00:05:45,090 --> 00:05:46,890
This is the reading room.

149
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:48,170
A chip like this one,

150
00:05:48,170 --> 00:05:50,870
made by an American company called Nvidia.

151
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:52,350
It's called a G.P.U.,

152
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:55,340
and a single one costs lakhs of rupees.

153
00:05:55,370 --> 00:05:56,880
And to train a big A.I.,

154
00:05:56,880 --> 00:05:59,570
you need thousands of them, wired together,

155
00:05:59,570 --> 00:06:01,050
running for months.

156
00:06:01,080 --> 00:06:02,250
And here's the catch.

157
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:04,320
India doesn't make these chips.

158
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:07,640
They're designed in America and made mostly in Taiwan.

159
00:06:07,670 --> 00:06:10,070
If you watched our chip film, you know why.

160
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:12,750
In January twenty twenty-five,

161
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:16,120
India found out how fast that door can close.

162
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:18,000
Just days before leaving office,

163
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:20,650
America's government put out a new rule.

164
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:23,120
It sorted the world into three groups.

165
00:06:23,170 --> 00:06:25,920
Its closest allies could buy these chips freely.

166
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:27,630
China was blocked.

167
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:28,480
And India?

168
00:06:28,740 --> 00:06:30,630
India was put in the middle group,

169
00:06:30,630 --> 00:06:33,910
with a limit on how many of these chips it could get.

170
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:34,840
Four months later,

171
00:06:34,840 --> 00:06:37,550
the new American government scrapped that rule.

172
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:39,330
But India had seen it.

173
00:06:39,410 --> 00:06:41,800
The tap is in someone else's hand.

174
00:06:41,890 --> 00:06:43,440
So what would you do?

175
00:06:43,550 --> 00:06:46,480
India didn't wait to own a giant reading room.

176
00:06:46,530 --> 00:06:48,820
It rented one... and shared it.

177
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:50,380
Under the IndiaAI Mission,

178
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:53,910
private companies put their chips into one common pool.

179
00:06:53,940 --> 00:06:56,520
Indian startups and researchers rent time on them,

180
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,950
and the government pays part of the bill.

181
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:00,560
In January twenty twenty-five,

182
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:04,240
India lined up more than eighteen thousand chips for that pool.

183
00:07:04,320 --> 00:07:05,810
By June twenty twenty-six,

184
00:07:05,810 --> 00:07:07,850
more than forty-five thousand.

185
00:07:07,880 --> 00:07:09,440
That's a big jump, right?

186
00:07:09,470 --> 00:07:11,590
But by the end of twenty twenty-four,

187
00:07:11,590 --> 00:07:13,130
one American company, Meta,

188
00:07:13,130 --> 00:07:17,650
said it would have around three hundred and fifty thousand of these chips.

189
00:07:17,680 --> 00:07:21,820
Almost eight times your country's whole shared pool this June.

190
00:07:21,850 --> 00:07:22,710
And the money?

191
00:07:22,740 --> 00:07:26,440
India's whole A.I. mission is about ten thousand crore rupees,

192
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:28,040
spread over five years.

193
00:07:28,070 --> 00:07:29,100
This year alone,

194
00:07:29,100 --> 00:07:32,540
Meta told its investors it would spend around thirteen

195
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:35,350
lakh crore rupees on buildings and machines,

196
00:07:35,350 --> 00:07:36,910
much of it for A.I.

197
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:42,010
So India's whole mission... ten thousand crore,

198
00:07:42,010 --> 00:07:43,340
over five years.

199
00:07:43,470 --> 00:07:45,930
Meta alone... thirteen lakh crore,

200
00:07:45,930 --> 00:07:47,650
in one single year.

201
00:07:47,820 --> 00:07:48,900
That's not double.

202
00:07:48,930 --> 00:07:51,180
That's more than a hundred times as much.

203
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:53,080
So is it hopeless?

204
00:07:53,200 --> 00:07:53,820
No.

205
00:07:53,870 --> 00:07:55,890
Because remember DeepSeek.

206
00:07:55,920 --> 00:08:00,030
DeepSeek says it trained its base model on about two thousand chips.

207
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:01,750
Not hundreds of thousands.

208
00:08:01,780 --> 00:08:03,540
And they were a cut-down version,

209
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:06,120
made so they could still be sold to China.

210
00:08:06,170 --> 00:08:08,380
So what's the lesson for you and me?

211
00:08:08,440 --> 00:08:10,860
You don't always need the biggest room.

212
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:12,750
You need clever engineering.

213
00:08:12,840 --> 00:08:15,440
And that's a game Indian engineers can play.

214
00:08:15,470 --> 00:08:17,120
April twenty twenty-five.

215
00:08:17,190 --> 00:08:21,750
The government picks the first company to build India's own model from scratch.

216
00:08:21,780 --> 00:08:24,070
A Bengaluru startup called Sarvam.

217
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:26,820
Sarvam was started by two Indians you should know,

218
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:29,000
Vivek Raghavan and Pratyush Kumar.

219
00:08:29,030 --> 00:08:33,040
The mission gives them about four thousand of those Nvidia chips to work with.

220
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,740
But a month later, the doubts begin.

221
00:08:35,770 --> 00:08:39,390
Sarvam's next model is built on top of a French one.

222
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:42,160
And critics ask — if the base is foreign,

223
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:43,640
how is it Indian?

224
00:08:43,730 --> 00:08:45,060
Fair question, isn't it?

225
00:08:45,090 --> 00:08:46,250
You'd ask it too.

226
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:48,820
Can an Indian team train one from zero,

227
00:08:48,820 --> 00:08:50,790
start to finish, at home?

228
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:52,590
February twenty twenty-six.

229
00:08:52,620 --> 00:08:54,150
Bharat Mandapam, Delhi.

230
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:56,820
The India A.I. Impact Summit.

231
00:08:56,850 --> 00:08:59,280
There, Sarvam shows India two new models.

232
00:08:59,310 --> 00:09:02,940
The bigger one has a hundred and five billion parameters.

233
00:09:02,970 --> 00:09:06,980
Think of them as tiny dials the machine tunes while it reads.

234
00:09:07,640 --> 00:09:09,170
Trained from scratch.

235
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,150
In India.

236
00:09:10,250 --> 00:09:12,490
On those shared chips you just saw.

237
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:14,490
And before it ever talked to you,

238
00:09:14,490 --> 00:09:17,420
it read around twelve trillion pieces of text.

239
00:09:17,650 --> 00:09:20,140
Then Sarvam did something generous.

240
00:09:20,190 --> 00:09:21,960
It made the model open.

241
00:09:22,090 --> 00:09:25,910
Any Indian developer can download it and build their own apps on it,

242
00:09:25,910 --> 00:09:26,760
for free.

243
00:09:26,930 --> 00:09:29,110
The minister had hoped for six months,

244
00:09:29,110 --> 00:09:31,240
and later said ten at the most.

245
00:09:31,460 --> 00:09:32,750
It took about a year.

246
00:09:32,990 --> 00:09:35,970
Late, you could say... but delivered.

247
00:09:36,170 --> 00:09:38,070
And that same week, BharatGen —

248
00:09:38,070 --> 00:09:39,850
a team led by I.I.T. Bombay —

249
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:42,480
showed its own model too, called Param Two,

250
00:09:42,480 --> 00:09:46,020
built for all twenty-two scheduled Indian languages.

251
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:49,010
Now remember my promise to you.

252
00:09:49,100 --> 00:09:50,320
The chopping test.

253
00:09:50,400 --> 00:09:51,430
Same question.

254
00:09:51,490 --> 00:09:53,420
Will it rain in my village tomorrow?

255
00:09:53,450 --> 00:09:55,470
DeepSeek's chopper, you've seen.

256
00:09:55,570 --> 00:09:56,860
Hindi, eighteen.

257
00:09:56,900 --> 00:09:58,320
Marathi, twenty.

258
00:09:58,410 --> 00:09:59,920
Telugu, twenty-five.

259
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:02,780
The chopper behind Chat G.P.T.'s recent models...

260
00:10:02,810 --> 00:10:06,370
Hindi eight, Marathi fourteen, Telugu fifteen.

261
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:07,450
And Sarvam's?

262
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:10,370
Hindi, eight — exactly the same as English.

263
00:10:10,480 --> 00:10:11,780
Marathi, ten.

264
00:10:11,830 --> 00:10:13,180
Telugu, eleven.

265
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:16,300
And this isn't one lucky sentence.

266
00:10:16,330 --> 00:10:19,600
We ran the same test on more than a thousand sentences,

267
00:10:19,600 --> 00:10:21,800
each one in thirteen languages.

268
00:10:21,830 --> 00:10:22,880
Look at Odia.

269
00:10:22,990 --> 00:10:24,110
On DeepSeek's chopper,

270
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:26,710
five and a half times the pieces of English.

271
00:10:26,850 --> 00:10:29,320
On Sarvam's... less than one and a half.

272
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:32,070
And BharatGen's chopper does about as well.

273
00:10:32,120 --> 00:10:35,500
So for your language, the gap shrinks, a lot.

274
00:10:35,540 --> 00:10:39,110
Because this time... an Indian team put the missing pieces in.

275
00:10:39,930 --> 00:10:41,600
And it's already at work.

276
00:10:41,720 --> 00:10:46,300
Sarvam says its models help run Aadhaar services in Indian languages.

277
00:10:46,380 --> 00:10:47,250
And in October,

278
00:10:47,250 --> 00:10:52,920
Maharashtra gave Sarvam's A.I. assistant to two thousand five hundred government officials.

279
00:10:53,010 --> 00:10:56,130
So has India built its own Chat G.P.T.?

280
00:10:56,290 --> 00:10:57,700
Let me be honest with you.

281
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:00,330
Not one that matches the best in the world.

282
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:01,170
Not yet.

283
00:11:01,200 --> 00:11:06,400
DeepSeek's newest big model has one point six trillion of those dials in total.

284
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:08,400
About fifteen times Sarvam's.

285
00:11:08,430 --> 00:11:11,170
And Sarvam says it plainly, in its own report.

286
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:15,430
The newest models from other labs are trained on far bigger clusters,

287
00:11:15,430 --> 00:11:17,240
with far bigger budgets.

288
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:18,800
Here's the real scoreboard.

289
00:11:18,910 --> 00:11:20,770
India has the teachers.

290
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:23,040
India has started on the library.

291
00:11:23,160 --> 00:11:24,440
And for the reading room...

292
00:11:24,620 --> 00:11:26,770
India still rents the chips.

293
00:11:26,860 --> 00:11:27,810
What's next?

294
00:11:27,840 --> 00:11:32,310
The IndiaAI Mission has now picked twenty Indian proposals to build models,

295
00:11:32,310 --> 00:11:34,560
Sarvam and BharatGen among them.

296
00:11:34,590 --> 00:11:37,260
And BharatGen's mandate is to keep scaling,

297
00:11:37,260 --> 00:11:39,790
all the way up to a trillion dials.

298
00:11:39,840 --> 00:11:41,330
So here's the lesson.

299
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:45,630
A machine speaks best in the language of the people who build it.

300
00:11:45,660 --> 00:11:48,770
If India only rents other countries' machines,

301
00:11:48,770 --> 00:11:54,090
you and I will keep paying extra to be understood... in our own languages.

302
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:54,770
But today,

303
00:11:54,770 --> 00:11:57,900
there's an Indian machine that reads your Hindi,

304
00:11:57,900 --> 00:11:58,940
Marathi, Telugu,

305
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:03,320
Bengali or Odia more cheaply than Chat G.P.T. and DeepSeek do.

306
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:05,420
So what does this mean for you?

307
00:12:05,450 --> 00:12:05,800
First,

308
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:09,240
check your Chat G.P.T. plan before the free year ends.

309
00:12:09,270 --> 00:12:10,140
Once it's over,

310
00:12:10,140 --> 00:12:12,630
it can start charging you on its own.

311
00:12:13,310 --> 00:12:15,270
And before you pay for any A.I.,

312
00:12:15,270 --> 00:12:19,710
ask the same question in your own language to two or three of them.

313
00:12:19,870 --> 00:12:22,660
Chat G.P.T., DeepSeek, and an Indian one,

314
00:12:22,660 --> 00:12:25,180
like Sarvam's app, called Indus.

315
00:12:25,460 --> 00:12:28,640
Pay only for the one that really understands you.

316
00:12:28,860 --> 00:12:32,520
And A.I. isn't the only thing India has built for itself.

317
00:12:32,780 --> 00:12:36,080
Did you know India has its own satellite navigation,

318
00:12:36,080 --> 00:12:37,190
up in space?

319
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:38,830
It's called NavIC.

320
00:12:38,970 --> 00:12:42,930
So why does your phone still lean on America's G.P.S.?

321
00:12:43,100 --> 00:12:44,610
That's our next story.

322
00:12:44,790 --> 00:12:47,020
Before you go, try this tonight.

323
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:50,320
Ask any A.I. one question in your mother tongue... and

324
00:12:50,320 --> 00:12:53,550
tell me in the comments which one answered best.

325
00:12:53,610 --> 00:12:55,050
I read every one.

326
00:12:55,230 --> 00:12:57,370
This is Be Practical with Kishore.

327
00:12:57,440 --> 00:12:58,500
Stay practical.

328
00:12:58,540 --> 00:12:59,260
Jai Hind.
